Algorithm / Procedure
Part A: Installation of Wireshark
Step‑1: Open a web browser and navigate to the official Wireshark website: https://www.wireshark.org/
Step‑2: Click on the Download button and select the Windows installer (64‑bit or 32‑bit as per system).
Step‑3: Save the installer file and run it.
Step‑4: Follow the installation wizard:

Accept the license agreement.
Select the installation directory (default is fine).
Select components: Wireshark, TShark, plugins, and tools.
Important: Install Npcap or WinPcap when prompted (required for packet capture).
Complete the installation. Step‑5: Launch Wireshark from the Start Menu or desktop shortcut.
For Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):

bash
v1.0
sudo apt update
sudo apt install wireshark
During installation, you may be prompted to allow non‑root users to capture packets. Select "Yes" if you want to allow this.

Part B: Packet Capture Using Wireshark (GUI)
Step‑1: Launch Wireshark.
Step‑2: In the main window, a list of available network interfaces is displayed. The interface with traffic activity will show a graph of packets.
Step‑3: Click on the interface name (e.g., Ethernet, Wi-Fi) or select Capture → Interfaces to see all interfaces.
Step‑4: Click the Start button (blue shark fin) to begin capturing packets.
Step‑5: The capture dialog will show the number of packets increasing as traffic flows.
Step‑6: Once sufficient packets have been captured (or after reproducing the problem to be analyzed), click the Stop button (red square).
Step‑7: The captured packets will appear in the Packet List pane.
Step‑8: To save the capture, go to File → Save As and choose a location and filename (default format .pcapng).

Part C: Starting Wireshark from Command Line
Step‑1: Open Command Prompt (Windows) or Terminal (Linux).
Step‑2: View help and command-line options:

bash
v1.0
wireshark -h
Step‑3: Capture packets from a specific interface with a capture filter:

bash
v1.0
wireshark -i <interface> -f "host 192.168.1.10" -w capture.pcap
Step‑4: Open a saved capture file:

bash
v1.0
wireshark -r capture.pcap
Step‑5: Apply a display filter when opening:

bash
v1.0
wireshark -r capture.pcap -Y "http"
Part D: Viewing Captured Traffic
Step‑1: In the Packet List pane, click on any packet to select it.
Step‑2: The Packet Details pane will expand, showing the protocol layers of the selected packet (e.g., Frame, Ethernet, IP, TCP, HTTP).
Step‑3: Click on the + signs to expand each layer and view detailed fields (source/destination addresses, ports, flags, etc.).
Step‑4: The Packet Bytes pane displays the raw hexadecimal and ASCII representation of the packet.
Step‑5: Use the scroll wheel or scrollbar to navigate through the packet list.

Part E: Applying Filters
Step‑1: In the Display Filter bar (above the Packet List pane), type a filter expression.

Example: To show only HTTP traffic:

http
Example: To show traffic from a specific IP:

ip.src == 192.168.1.10
Step‑2: Press Enter or click the Apply arrow. The Packet List will update to show only matching packets.
Step‑3: To clear the filter, click the X button next to the filter bar.

Capture Filters:

Go to Capture → Options.
In the Capture Filter field, enter a BPF filter (e.g., host 192.168.1.10).
Click Start to begin capture with the filter applied.
Part F: Analysis and Statistics
Step‑1: Go to the Statistics menu to access various analysis tools.

Tool	Description
Protocol Hierarchy	Displays a breakdown of protocols in the capture (percentage of packets/bytes per protocol).
Endpoints	Shows all endpoints (MAC, IP, ports) and their traffic statistics.
Conversations	Shows traffic between pairs of endpoints.
IO Graphs	Plots traffic over time (packets/sec, bytes/sec).
Flow Graph	Visualizes the sequence of packets between hosts.
TCP Stream Graph	Analyzes TCP streams (timing, throughput, etc.).
Packet Lengths	Displays the distribution of packet lengths.
Step‑2: Click on Statistics → Protocol Hierarchy to view the breakdown.
Step‑3: Click on Statistics → Endpoints to see all communicating endpoints.
Step‑4: Click on Statistics → Conversations to see traffic between host pairs.

Part G: Following TCP Streams
Step‑1: Right‑click on a TCP packet in the Packet List pane.
Step‑2: Select Follow → TCP Stream.
Step‑3: A new window opens showing the entire TCP conversation in both directions.
Step‑4: You can view the data in ASCII, HEX, or other formats.
Step‑5: This is useful for reconstructing HTTP requests, FTP transfers, and other application‑layer traffic.

Part H: Using Colouring Rules
Step‑1: Go to View → Coloring Rules.
Step‑2: Wireshark has default color rules for common protocols (e.g., TCP is blue, UDP is light blue, HTTP is green).
Step‑3: To add a custom coloring rule:

Click New.
Enter a name and a display filter expression.
Choose a foreground and background color.
Click OK.
Examples
Example 1: Capturing HTTP Traffic Only
Capture Filter:

port 80
Display Filter:

http
Example 2: Capturing Traffic to/from a Specific IP
Capture Filter:

host 192.168.1.10
Display Filter:

ip.addr == 192.168.1.10
Example 3: Following a TCP Stream (HTTP GET Request)
Capture traffic while browsing a website.
In the Packet List, find an HTTP GET packet.
Right‑click → Follow → TCP Stream.
The stream shows the full HTTP request and response.
Example 4: Command-Line Capture
bash
v1.0
wireshark -i eth0 -f "tcp port 80" -w http_traffic.pcap
Constraints
Wireshark requires administrative/root privileges to capture packets.
In switched networks, only the capturing computer's own traffic is visible unless port mirroring is enabled.
Capturing all traffic on a busy network can generate very large files – use capture filters to reduce data.
Analyzing encrypted traffic (HTTPS, SSH, etc.) requires decryption keys (which are not available in typical scenarios).
